<p>Tensions between Washington and Madrid are rising as Spain reiterates it will not allow US forces to use two jointly operated bases in southern Spain for Iran-related military operations as the conflict escalates. President Trump has responded with sharp public criticism of Spain’s leadership and warnings of punitive economic steps. Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez has been unusually outspoken among European leaders, publicly challenging the US approach to Iran, according to multiple outlets’ accounts of his messaging and political posture. The dispute is also testing how much friction NATO allies can absorb as the Iran war intensifies.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/H9PhK9d3-INO-B7weDncip3wZM8Wt2lhcjR_HcUKfYejEauNK7EJBxXxgB40VGTZ9HWgvJlNGyT8yKZ08A61MEbttl7sARxnXRaHj9L9WSdDg9FYW139yRet9EsES6Wngl7ChK6KFEJdgLo6KYBJooTPP1HYcxqMakjHC4EISjgYcLfHkI626z2REMlqsFVW7Zf4cpRg9v2zE1rtq4d6j7-t7vikjkG0viZjZaT7sTjPk7NcL1txsNwui7dOBLHbtL32eG38zFMrh9C-zw' alt='Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez hits back at U.S. President Donald Trump&#x27;s criticisms of Madrid&#x27;s refusal to let U.S. planes use its bases to attack Iran, saying &#x27;no to war.&#x27;' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Bases at issue: Spain’s refusal centers on Morón Air Base and Naval Station Rota, both jointly used with the United States.</li><li>White House dispute: Spain’s foreign minister publicly pushed back on a White House press office claim about Spain’s position on the bases, escalating the diplomatic back-and-forth.</li><li>Escalating rhetoric: Trump’s criticism of Madrid has included personal insults and warnings that the United States will stop acting as a cooperative “team player” toward Spain.</li><li>Public messaging: After Trump’s initial threat, Sánchez delivered a televised address framed as an “institutional declaration” to assess international events, laying out Spain’s position as the row grew.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Spanish government (Pedro Sánchez): Sánchez has cast Spain as opposing escalation in Iran and positioned himself as one of the most vocal European leaders willing to confront Trump publicly over the conflict. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-spains-prime-minister-is-making-a-career-of-not-bending-a-knee-to/'>The Globe and Mail</a>, <a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/06/pedro-sanchez-spain-european-leader-take-on-trump'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>United States (President Trump): Trump has publicly threatened punitive economic measures against Spain, including saying he would “cut off all trade with Spain,” and separately warning of a trade “embargo.” (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/middle-east/article/3345676/european-leader-defying-trump-and-his-attacks-iran'>South China Morning Post</a>, <a href='https://english.elpais.com/usa/2026-03-05/trump-calls-spain-a-loser-and-warns-the-united-states-will-not-be-a-team-player.html'>El País</a>)</li><li>Deutsche Welle framing: DW described the dispute as a major strain in US-Spain relations during the Iran war and asked how far the confrontation could go, including whether either side might sever diplomatic ties. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/iran-war-strains-us-spain-relations/a-76234578'>DW</a>)</li><li>NDTV analysis: NDTV argued Trump’s Iran campaign has increased his practical need for European backing after years of complaining that Europe was complacent and sheltered under America’s security umbrella. (<a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-iran-israel-war-trump-finds-he-needs-europe-now-that-hes-waging-war-in-iran-11175833#publisher=newsstand'>NDTV</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/middle-east/article/3345676/european-leader-defying-trump-and-his-attacks-iran'>The European leader defying Trump and his attacks on Iran</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-iran-israel-war-trump-finds-he-needs-europe-now-that-hes-waging-war-in-iran-1